class Solution {
private:
    void build(int n, int idx, string curr, vector<string>&ans){
        if(idx >= n){
            ans.push_back(curr);
            return;
        }

        // decided not to take 0
        build(n, idx + 1, curr, ans);

        //  decided to take 0;
        curr[idx] = '1';
        build(n, idx + 2, curr, ans);
        curr[idx] = '0';
    }
public:
    vector<string> generateBinaryStrings(int n) {
       vector<string>ans;
        string s(n, '0');
        build(n, 0, s, ans);

        return ans;
    }
};
```

## Complexity

**Time Complexity:**
O(Fibonacci(n)) — more precisely, the number of leaves in the recursion tree equals the number of binary strings of length `n` with no two consecutive 1's, which follows the Fibonacci recurrence `T(n) = T(n-1) + T(n-2)` (skip-1 vs place-1-and-skip-2). This count grows as `O(φⁿ)` where `φ ≈ 1.618` is the golden ratio. Each leaf also costs `O(n)` to copy `curr` into `ans` (string copy), so the total work is `O(n · φⁿ)`.

**Space Complexity:**
